use Libraries.Web.Page.all
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.ComputerScienceStandards2026
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.QuorumStandards

/*
    Tutorial: File Choosers, the computer's own windows for opening and
    saving files and choosing folders, in the user interface series. This
    lesson is offline only, so it has no block editor.
*/
class UserInterfacesFileChooser is QuorumPage
    // The site's sets of standards, built once for every page.
    QuorumStandards standards

    on create
        SetLocation("tutorials/ui/fileChooser.html")
        SetShortTitle("File Choosers")
        SetSectionName("User Interfaces")
    end

    action Setup
        // For search engines and link previews.
        SetDescription("Use Quorum's FileChooser to open a file, save a file and choose a folder from buttons on a Form, limit the choice with a FileFilter, and keep saved files safe with the CIA triad.")

        Title("File Choosers")

        Overview()
        WarmUp()
        Explore()
        Engage()
        WrapUp()
    end

    action Overview
        Section("Overview")
        Paragraph("Many programs let people open and save files: a text editor opens a story,
a drawing app saves a picture, a game loads a saved level. To do this, the
program needs to ask the person "):Strong("which"):Text(" file. A "):Strong("file chooser"):Text(" is the
window that lets them browse their folders and pick one. In Quorum,
the "):Code("FileChooser"):Text(" class opens the computer's own file window, the same one
other programs use, and gives your program back the file that was picked.
In this tutorial, you will open a file, save a file, choose a folder, and
limit which kinds of files can be picked, all from buttons on a page.")

        Paragraph():Strong("This lesson works only offline, in Quorum Studio on a desktop computer."):Text("
Unlike the other lessons in this series, its programs can't run in the
online code editor, because a program in a web browser can't open your
computer's file windows.")

        Subsection("Goals")
        Paragraph("You have the following goals for this tutorial:")
        Goals()
        ListItem("Open the computer's open, save and choose folder windows with "):Code("FileChooser")
        ListItem("Use the "):Code("File"):Text(" that comes back, and handle the case where the person
cancels")
        ListItem("Limit the kinds of files a person can pick with a "):Code("FileFilter")
        ListItem("Explain why Quorum uses the operating system's own file window")
        ListItem("Explain what can go wrong with saved files when confidentiality,
integrity or availability is ignored")
    end

    action WarmUp
        Section("Warm up")
        Paragraph("Think about the window that opens when you choose "):DoubleQuote():Text("Open"):DoubleQuote():Text(" in any app on your
computer. Is it the same in every app? Why might it be a good idea for
every app on a computer to use the same file window? Think about people who
use a keyboard or a screen reader. What would happen if every program made
its own?")

        Subsection("Vocabulary")
        Paragraph("You will be learning about the following vocabulary words:")
        Table("Vocabulary", "media/userInterface/FileChooser/Vocabulary.csv")

        Subsection("New Code to Learn")
        Paragraph("You will be using the following new pieces of code:")
        Table("New Code to Learn", "media/userInterface/FileChooser/Code.csv")

        Subsection("CSTA Standards")
        Paragraph("This tutorial covers the following "):Link("CSTA standards", "https://csteachers.org/pk12standards/view/"):Text(":")
        ComputerScienceStandards2026 computerScience = standards:GetComputerScience2026()
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_HARDWARE_AND_SOFTWARE_29)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:MIDDLE_SCHOOL_TESTING_AND_REFINING_19)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:SPECIALTY_1_SOFTWARE_DEVELOPMENT_TESTING_AND_REFINING_7)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:MIDDLE_SCHOOL_SECURITY_32)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_SECURITY_33)
        StandardsList(computerScience)
    end

    action Explore
        Section("Explore")

        FilesAndFileChoosers()
        OpeningAFile()
        SavingAFile()
        ChoosingAFolder()
        AddingTheButtonsToAPage()
        KeepingSavedFilesSafe()
    end

    private action FilesAndFileChoosers
        Subsection("Files and file choosers")
        Paragraph("In Quorum, the "):Code("File"):Text(" class stands for one file or folder on the computer.
A "):Code("File"):Text(" knows its name and its path, and it can read or write the text
inside the file. This tutorial uses only a few of its actions. The
"):Link("File documentation", "/Libraries/System/File.html"):Text(" lists the rest.")

        Paragraph("A "):Code("FileChooser"):Text(" does not draw its own window. Instead, it asks the
"):Strong("operating system"):Text(" to open the same file window that every other program
on the computer uses. This has two big benefits:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("People already know how to use it.")
        ListItem("It already works with the computer's screen reader and keyboard
shortcuts, so you don't have to build that yourself.")

        Paragraph("Because the window comes from the operating system, it looks different on
Windows and on macOS. Your Quorum code is the same on both.")

        Paragraph("File choosers work in Quorum Studio on a desktop computer. They don't work
in the online code editor, which can't reach the files on your computer.")

        Paragraph("There are three kinds of file window:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("Open"):Text(": pick a file that already exists.")
        ListItem():Strong("Save"):Text(": pick a folder and type a name for a file. The file might not
exist yet.")
        ListItem():Strong("Choose folder"):Text(": pick a folder instead of a file.")
    end

    private action OpeningAFile
        Subsection("Opening a file")
        Paragraph("In Quorum Studio, make a new project. Under "):Strong("General"):Text(", choose "):Strong("App"):Text(".
Each window will be opened by a button on the page, with its own behavior.
Add a class named "):Code("OpenFileBehavior.quorum"):Text(":")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/FileChooser/OpenFileBehavior.quorum")

        Paragraph("Here is what it does:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("The "):Code("FileFilter"):Text(" lists the file extensions the window should allow. Here,
it is only "):Code("txt"):Text(", so the person can pick text files. Write the extension
without the dot.")
        ListItem():Strong("A new filter allows every file."):Text(" Until you call "):Code("SetAcceptAll(false)"):Text(",
the extensions you added are not used at all. Always call it when you want
the filter to limit the choice.")
        ListItem():Code("OpenFileDialog(filter)"):Text(" opens the window and "):Strong("waits"):Text(" until the person
picks a file or cancels.")
        ListItem("If the person cancels, the result is "):Code("undefined"):Text(". Always check for this.
If you don't, your program stops with an error the first time someone
changes their mind.")
        ListItem():Code("file:Read()"):Text(" returns all the text in the file.")
    end

    private action SavingAFile
        Subsection("Saving a file")
        Paragraph("Add a class named "):Code("SaveFileBehavior.quorum"):Text(":")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/FileChooser/SaveFileBehavior.quorum")

        Paragraph("In a save window, the person types a new name, or picks a file that is
already there. "):Code("file:Exists()"):Text(" tells you which one happened. "):Code("file:Write"):Text("
creates the file if needed, and "):Strong("replaces"):Text(" everything in it if it was
already there. Most save windows warn the person before they replace a
file.")
    end

    private action ChoosingAFolder
        Subsection("Choosing a folder")
        Paragraph("Add a class named "):Code("ChooseFolderBehavior.quorum"):Text(":")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/FileChooser/ChooseFolderBehavior.quorum")

        Paragraph():Code("ChooseFolderDialog"):Text(" needs a "):Code("File"):Text(" that says which folder to start in. A new
"):Code("File"):Text(" that has no path refers to your program's working folder, so the
window starts there.")

        Paragraph("The open and save windows can take a starting folder too. Give the folder
first and the filter second:")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/FileChooser/OpenWithStart.quorum")

        Paragraph("A filter only makes sense for files, so "):Code("ChooseFolderDialog"):Text(" doesn't take one.")
    end

    private action AddingTheButtonsToAPage
        Subsection("Adding the buttons to a page")
        Paragraph("Now replace "):Code("Main.quorum"):Text(". It puts one button on the page for each window.")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/FileChooser/Main.quorum")

        Paragraph("The page stacks the buttons in rows and puts them in the "):Key("Tab"):Text(" order for
you, so a keyboard user can reach every one.")

        Paragraph("Run the program. Use the Save File button first to make a text file, then
open it with the Open File button. The output window shows what happened
each time.")
    end

    private action KeepingSavedFilesSafe
        Subsection("Keeping saved files safe")
        Paragraph("Security experts often check how data is protected with three ideas, called
the "):Strong("CIA triad"):Text(". "):Strong("Confidentiality"):Text(" means only the people who should read
information can read it. "):Strong("Integrity"):Text(" means information isn't changed or
destroyed in ways it shouldn't be. "):Strong("Availability"):Text(" means people can get to
their information when they need it
("):Link("source", "https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/security"):Text(").")

        Paragraph("Each one matters when a program saves files. Here is what can happen when a
program ignores them:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("Confidentiality:"):Text(" a person saves a private diary into a folder that
everyone on a shared computer can open, and anyone can read it. A program
can help by suggesting a private folder, and by never saving somewhere
the person didn't choose.")
        ListItem():Strong("Integrity:"):Text(" "):Code("file:Write"):Text(" replaces everything in a file that is already
there. If the program never checks "):Code("file:Exists()"):Text(" and asks first, one
wrong click or key press destroys someone's work. The
"):Link("Option Dialogs", "/tutorials/ui/optionDialog.html"):Text(" tutorial shows how to
ask.")
        ListItem():Strong("Availability:"):Text(" if the only copy of a file is on one drive, and that
drive breaks or is lost, the file is gone. Keeping a backup copy
somewhere else means the work can still be reached.")
    end

    action Engage
        Section("Engage")
        Paragraph("In this activity, you write a "):Strong("test plan"):Text(" for the file chooser program,
run it, and record what really happens. The goal is to
think about everything a person might do with a file chooser, including the
unexpected, before you trust your program. A test plan is a list of things
to try and what should happen for each. Include at least these cases:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("picking a file, then pressing Cancel instead;")
        ListItem("saving with a new name, and saving over a file that's already there;")
        ListItem("trying to open a file that isn't a "):Code(".txt"):Text(" file;")
        ListItem("one test for each part of the CIA triad, such as saving into a folder
other people can open, or saving over a file with the only copy of
your work;")
        ListItem("doing everything with only the keyboard ("):Key("Tab"):Text(", "):Key("Space"):Text(", "):Key("Enter"):Text(", the
arrow keys and "):Key("Escape"):Text(").")

        Paragraph("Run each test and write down what really happened next to what you
expected. If you can, run the same tests on a second operating system, and
write down what was different about its file window. Did anything surprise
you? For each CIA test, write what the person would lose if the program
got it wrong. Then pick one of the made-up flaws from "):DoubleQuote():Text("Keeping saved files
safe"):DoubleQuote():Text(", such as the diary saved in a shared folder, and write a fix for it:
what the program should do differently, and which part of the CIA triad
your fix protects. Fix one problem you found, or change the program so it
saves text that the person typed into a text field on the page. The
"):Link("Text Fields tutorial", "/tutorials/ui/textField.html"):Text(" shows how to get typed
text.")
    end

    action WrapUp
        Section("Wrap up")
        Paragraph("Your program used a window it didn't build. The operating system made it.
What are the good and bad sides of handing part of your program to the
operating system? What can you no longer control?")
    end
end
